<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">French Senegal</h1><p class="st-meta">1659 CE – 1960 CE · capital Saint-Louis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's oldest colony in sub-Saharan Africa, which grew from a trading post at Saint-Louis into the political centre of French West Africa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>French merchants founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis</a> in 1659 on an island at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> river, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/goree">Goree</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in 1677. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartered-company">Chartered companies</a> traded upriver for gum arabic, gold and slaves, with forts as far as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gajaaga">Galam</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> occupied the posts during wars in 1758-1779 and 1809-1817 before they returned to France.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe">Louis Faidherbe</a>, governor in 1854-1861 and 1863-1865, pushed French rule inland, fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/toucouleur-empire">Toucouleur Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cayor">Cayor</a>, and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-communes">Four Communes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/goree">Goree</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rufisque">Rufisque</a> and Dakar gave their inhabitants French citizenship, and in 1914 they elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blaise-diagne">Blaise Diagne</a>, the first black African in the French parliament. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">Groundnuts</a> became the main export, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> brotherhoods such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mouride-brotherhood">Mourides</a> shaped rural society.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> became an autonomous republic within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a> in 1958, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-federation">Mali Federation</a> in 1959, and became fully independent on 20 August 1960 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-sedar-senghor">Leopold Sedar Senghor</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a>, capital of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa</a>, remained the capital of the new state.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-senegal.jpg" alt="French Senegal" width="320" height="193"><figcaption>Image: Guillaume de L'Isle · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guillaume_Delisle_Senegambia_1707.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1659 CE – 1960 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saint-Louis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Andre Brue, Louis Faidherbe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Sufi orders), Catholicism among settlers</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Wolof</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Senegal</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of Senegal, Senegal (colony)</dd></dl></aside></div>
